WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:13.460
So now I'd like to
introduce today's speakers.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:18.160
Today's featured speakers
are Ron Fedkiw and Gordon Wetzstein.

00:00:18.168 --> 00:00:21.910
Ron Fedkiw is a professor of
Computer Science at Stanford University.

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:24.386
He received his PhD in
Mathematics from UCLA.

00:00:24.386 --> 00:00:27.370
And his post-doctoral studies
were at UCLA in mathematics and

00:00:27.370 --> 00:00:30.490
at Caltech in aeronautics
before joining Stanford.

00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:33.962
He was awarded an Academy Award from
the Academy of Motion Picture Arts and

00:00:33.962 --> 00:00:38.051
Sciences twice, the National Academy of
Science Award for Initiatives in Research,

00:00:38.051 --> 00:00:41.470
a Packard Foundation fellowship,
Presidential Early Career Award for

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:44.459
Scientists and Engineers,
along with several other awards.

00:00:44.459 --> 00:00:48.447
He is currently on the editorial board of
the Journal of Computational Physics and

00:00:48.447 --> 00:00:51.741
he participates in the reviewing
process of a number of journals and

00:00:51.741 --> 00:00:52.745
funding agencies.

00:00:52.745 --> 00:00:56.869
He has published about 120 research
papers in computational physics,

00:00:56.869 --> 00:01:00.750
computer graphics and vision,
as well as a book on level set methods.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:04.498
For the past 17 years, he has been
a consultant with Industria Light + Magic.

00:01:04.498 --> 00:01:09.510
He received screen credits for movies such
as Terminator 3, Star Wars Episode 3,

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:13.620
Poseidon, Evan Almighty, and
most recently on Kong Skull Island.

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:18.878
Gordon Wetzstein is an Assistant Professor
of Electrical Engineering and by courtesy,

00:01:18.878 --> 00:01:21.068
of Computer Science,
at Stanford University.

00:01:21.068 --> 00:01:24.601
He's a leader of
the Stanford Computational Imaging Lab,

00:01:24.601 --> 00:01:28.771
an interdisciplinary research group
focused on advancing imaging,

00:01:28.771 --> 00:01:30.832
microscopy and display systems.

00:01:30.832 --> 00:01:34.609
At the intersection of computer graphics,
machine vision optics,

00:01:34.609 --> 00:01:39.231
scientific computing and perception,
Professor Wetzstein's research has a wide

00:01:39.231 --> 00:01:44.114
range of applications in next generation
consumer electronics, scientific imaging,

00:01:44.114 --> 00:01:47.914
human computer interaction,
remote sensing and many other areas.

00:01:47.914 --> 00:01:51.875
He received a PhD in computer science from
the University of British Columbia and

00:01:51.875 --> 00:01:56.140
graduated with honors from the Bauhaus
in Weimar, Germany before that.

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:59.572
He was won the Alain Fournier
PhD Dissertation annual award,

00:01:59.572 --> 00:02:01.882
is the recipient of
an NSF CAREER award and

00:02:01.882 --> 00:02:06.634
has won Best Papers at the International
Conference on Computational Photography,

00:02:06.634 --> 00:02:08.624
as well as the Laval Virtual award.

00:02:08.624 --> 00:02:11.530
So both of them are quite accomplished.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:14.720
And I should also mention that they
are co-academic directors of our

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:18.295
new visual computing graduate certificate
which I will talk more about after

00:02:18.295 --> 00:02:19.342
their presentation.

00:02:19.342 --> 00:02:23.512
So now I'd like to first turn
the floor over to Gordon for

00:02:23.512 --> 00:02:26.283
the first part of the presentation.

00:02:27.761 --> 00:02:30.610
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much for
the introduction, Stephanie.

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:34.537
Time for visual computing, because there's
a lot of dynamics in both computer

00:02:34.537 --> 00:02:39.400
graphics, computer vision, but also in the
[INAUDIBLE] perception in virtual reality.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:43.486
So what we'd like to do today is walk
you through some of these topics so

00:02:43.486 --> 00:02:46.898
you get an overview of what
visual computing is all about.

00:02:46.898 --> 00:02:49.268
So Stephanie already introduced this, but

00:02:49.268 --> 00:02:53.980
I pulled up this nice photo of Ron where
he received a technical achievement award.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:57.390
And sorry about that, but
it was really a great achievement and

00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:01.130
he's done a lot of groundbreaking
work in simulation and

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:05.560
visual effects and is now working
on machine learning as well.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:09.220
I work more on the imaging side,
I also work on displays,

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:13.350
computer vision and
more recently on virtual reality as well.

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:18.560
So you may wonder what computational
imaging is all about and

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:20.190
all these topics are about.

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:22.590
So I just wanna walk you
through some of these and

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:25.610
show you some of the top
trends in these topics.

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:30.320
The computational imaging is usually
this idea of co-designing the optics,

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:33.340
the sensors, and
the algorithms for camera.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:38.640
And traditionally cameras have been
designed by designing the optics forming

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:43.760
an image on a digital sensor or in a film
play and then just looking at the image.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:48.540
But with the rise of computational power,
we can now co-design these things so

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:52.750
that the computer can understand the image
and extract meaningful information.

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:54.424
So what does it mean in practice?

00:03:54.424 --> 00:03:59.395
In practice that means the camera today
doesn't work similar to what a camera did

00:03:59.395 --> 00:04:03.710
a couple of hundred years ago,
even though it looks the same.

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:06.610
We now capture multiple images
with different exposures

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:10.750
every time that we hit the button, and
that's called high dynamic range imaging.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:13.710
The modes of your cellphone
cameras will actually capture

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:15.780
different images all at once.

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:19.730
The processor on the phone will
then compute a single image

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:23.630
that combines the most useful
information from all of these images.

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:26.231
And that's pretty much in standard mode.

00:04:26.231 --> 00:04:31.045
We can also think about super resolution,
so trying to resolve features in an image

00:04:31.045 --> 00:04:34.780
that are not visible in the resolution
offered by the detector.

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:37.220
And there are many other
imaging modalities,

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:40.960
like sending the depth of fields,
capturing light fields,

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:46.260
and doing more exotic imaging
modalities like compression imaging.

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:52.520
So this all sounds great, and
how does that translate into practice?

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:56.880
Well, we've seen a couple of startup
companies coming out of Stanford and

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:03.070
also here in the Silicon Valley,
such as Lytro which built the consumer

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:06.960
electronics version of the electrocamera
that you can buy in the store now.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:10.920
This camera allows you to do
things like post-capture refocus.

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:12.520
So you can take a picture and

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:16.160
if you got the focus wrong,
you can actually change it after the fact.

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:19.880
This camera also allows you to
extract depth information of a scene,

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:23.720
which is increasingly important for
things like autonomous driving,

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:26.240
consumer electronics, gaming,
and other applications.

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:29.920
Another consumer electronics
startup company,

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:33.400
just down the street here from Stanford,
is called Light.

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:37.738
Light has started to release
a camera that they call l16.

00:05:37.738 --> 00:05:41.466
It has 16 different
cellphone camera modules.

00:05:41.466 --> 00:05:45.670
You can see that here in the middle, and
they all have different focal lengths,

00:05:45.670 --> 00:05:49.624
different properties, and they can all
snap a single picture of a scene and

00:05:49.624 --> 00:05:53.139
then the computer will actually
figure out how to merge all of these

00:05:53.139 --> 00:05:55.712
images into getting a really
wide field of view,

00:05:55.712 --> 00:05:59.220
super high resolution image with
unprecedented image quality.

00:06:00.605 --> 00:06:03.140
Light cameras aren't just meant for
consumer electronics.

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:06.720
They also enable new experiences,
for example, in virtual reality.

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:10.425
On the right you can see
Facebook's new 360 camera,

00:06:10.425 --> 00:06:15.425
captures images in the 360
degrees sphere and can then

00:06:15.425 --> 00:06:20.265
process these to fuse them together into
a seamless virtual reality experience.

00:06:20.265 --> 00:06:24.245
So this also ties in back to enabling new

00:06:24.245 --> 00:06:28.267
types of experiences with these imaging
systems, for example, in virtual reality.

00:06:28.267 --> 00:06:32.770
So it turns out that the similar idea
can also be applied to displays.

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:37.160
This code design of algorithms and
unconventional optical systems

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:42.010
together with focusing on understanding
human beyond the visual system.

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:46.251
And a couple of research ideas that people
have been working on are high dynamic

00:06:46.251 --> 00:06:51.344
range displays, super resolution displays,
new types of projectors or displays.

00:06:51.344 --> 00:06:55.599
And of course, we all are also interested
in developing the next generation of

00:06:55.599 --> 00:06:57.603
head mounted displays for AR and VR.

00:06:57.603 --> 00:07:00.656
So I wanna talk about one of these
technologies a little bit more,

00:07:00.656 --> 00:07:03.767
which has already migrated into
the consumer electronics world,

00:07:03.767 --> 00:07:06.600
and that's high dynamic range displays.

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:11.200
So when I did my PhD about ten years ago,
that was a really nice research project

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:14.210
with a couple of papers,
things that you'd learn in a course.

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:17.430
But now you can actually buy
a television at Best Buy or

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:20.520
anywhere else that has that
technology integrated.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:22.060
And every company has a different name for
it.

00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:24.880
For example,
local dimming is what Samsung calls it.

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:29.310
And the basic idea is that you can
modulate the backlight of the television,

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:32.320
the LEDs that are actually
inside the television

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:35.170
together with the LCD that
is usually being used

00:07:35.170 --> 00:07:39.040
to create a contrast that is much higher
that what you get with a normal display.

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:43.461
So that creates a much more
lifelike experience, and

00:07:43.461 --> 00:07:47.300
much better user experience overall, okay.

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:51.336
So the basic idea between behind
computational imaging, for

00:07:51.336 --> 00:07:56.701
me it's this bridge between optics and
modern signal processing optimization.

00:07:56.701 --> 00:07:58.860
So we mostly work with geometric optics so

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:02.585
we think about light as a wave
travelling in straight lines in space.

00:08:02.585 --> 00:08:06.613
My research group also does a lot
of work on using wave optics and

00:08:06.613 --> 00:08:11.109
more complicated optical models
that are more physically accurate.

00:08:11.109 --> 00:08:14.473
But for the most part in
computer vision and imaging,

00:08:14.473 --> 00:08:17.553
it's good enough to think
about light as waves.

00:08:17.553 --> 00:08:23.160
And then use modern techniques in
optimization, deep learning, machine

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:28.970
learning and so on to design new kinds
of devices such as cameras and this bot.

00:08:28.970 --> 00:08:32.760
And these have applications in so
many different domains.

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:36.362
Every one of you probably has a whole
bunch of cameras already in your pocket.

00:08:36.362 --> 00:08:39.480
Cuz your cellphone likely
has at least two of them.

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:43.920
But we also think about new and
emerging depth cameras for

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:47.680
consumer electronic applications,
or cameras on drones.

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:50.790
We can think about surveillance,
medical imaging.

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:56.155
Cameras are in space, they're being used
for biomedical applications in microscopy.

00:08:56.155 --> 00:09:00.462
And they're being used, of course,
also for emerging autonomous vehicles or

00:09:00.462 --> 00:09:05.620
self-driving cars, where the computer has
to make the decision of what to do next.

00:09:05.620 --> 00:09:08.733
So, designing the sensors for these
kinds of systems is absolutely critical.

00:09:10.856 --> 00:09:13.121
So now I'd like to think about
computer vision a little bit more and

00:09:13.121 --> 00:09:14.700
how it's different from imaging.

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:16.560
Imaging is very close to the hardware.

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:20.540
We design algorithms and hardware
together, but at a very low level.

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:24.080
Computer vision is more concerned
with inferring high level

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:25.120
information of the scene.

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:29.360
Such as, you give me a couple
of images of your living room.

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:33.122
And the algorithm can figure out
what the 3D shape, and the texture,

00:09:33.122 --> 00:09:34.060
and the colors are.

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:36.286
So that we can use that in, for example,

00:09:36.286 --> 00:09:40.035
in an online program where we can
place new furnitures in your room.

00:09:40.035 --> 00:09:43.060
And you can try out different
arrangements of your room.

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:45.901
You can order furniture
from IKEA online and

00:09:45.901 --> 00:09:48.756
place them directly into your living room.

00:09:48.756 --> 00:09:52.425
At the same time these techniques are also
useful for autonomous driving, for

00:09:52.425 --> 00:09:55.580
robotic vision, and for
many different other fields.

00:09:55.580 --> 00:10:00.907
But in general computer vision is
concerned with high level decision making,

00:10:00.907 --> 00:10:04.389
or inference on data that
is captured with camera.

00:10:05.936 --> 00:10:11.770
So another topic that comes up in computer
vision quite a lot is scene understanding.

00:10:11.770 --> 00:10:14.769
So given an image, what's going on?

00:10:14.769 --> 00:10:18.900
So if we look at the upper left image
here, this is a picture taken from a car.

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:20.867
The car wants to understand
where the lanes are.

00:10:20.867 --> 00:10:25.340
It wants to understand where other
cars are, how fast they are driving.

00:10:25.340 --> 00:10:28.720
On the lower left we see
an example of segmentation.

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:32.080
We wanna understand,
is it a pedestrian we're looking at?

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:33.160
Is it a bicyclist?

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:34.995
Is it a driver?

00:10:34.995 --> 00:10:35.917
Is it a building?

00:10:35.917 --> 00:10:37.500
Is it a dynamic part of the scene?

00:10:37.500 --> 00:10:38.633
And so on.

00:10:38.633 --> 00:10:43.510
So segmentation, object tracking,
object understanding and recognition,

00:10:43.510 --> 00:10:47.060
these are key challenges
in computer vision.

00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:51.958
And on the lower right, you see a classic
example where we take an image and

00:10:51.958 --> 00:10:55.359
the computer will figure
out which object is what.

00:10:55.359 --> 00:10:58.613
And try to find it also in
a database of existing objects.

00:10:58.613 --> 00:11:01.994
So for example, we can say hey,
on the upper left there's an airplane.

00:11:01.994 --> 00:11:05.018
On the lower left there's a human,
and maybe we can even so

00:11:05.018 --> 00:11:06.698
some face recognition on that.

00:11:06.698 --> 00:11:08.895
So these are all graphical
computer vision problems.

00:11:10.410 --> 00:11:11.829
Tracking is another one.

00:11:11.829 --> 00:11:14.921
So we wanna be able to predict
the path of the motion for

00:11:14.921 --> 00:11:17.205
different types of objects in a scene.

00:11:19.487 --> 00:11:21.890
So computer graphics,
I don't wanna talk too much about it.

00:11:21.890 --> 00:11:23.340
We've learned a lot about teapots, and

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:26.225
bunnies, and
strange rooms with reflective objects.

00:11:26.225 --> 00:11:28.731
Juan will talk a lot more about this, but

00:11:28.731 --> 00:11:33.819
computer graphics is the science of taking
a mathematical description of light,

00:11:33.819 --> 00:11:36.842
reflectant models of
geometry of the scene, and

00:11:36.842 --> 00:11:42.027
computing an image of this that is ideally
indistinguishable from the real world.

00:11:42.027 --> 00:11:47.970
Another emerging area is virtual reality,
and augmented reality of course, too.

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:50.319
It's an area that's been around for
a few decades already.

00:11:50.319 --> 00:11:55.042
But in the last few years we've made so
much progress and we now have the consumer

00:11:55.042 --> 00:11:59.033
electronic devices that deliver
really immersive experiences.

00:11:59.033 --> 00:12:02.809
And virtual reality is being used for
many different applications,

00:12:02.809 --> 00:12:06.792
from simulation and training,
to entertainment, to visualization.

00:12:06.792 --> 00:12:10.320
But you shouldn't forget about
robotic surgery for example, too.

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:12.967
On the lower right, you see the da Vinci,

00:12:12.967 --> 00:12:15.550
a minimally invasive
robotic surgery system.

00:12:15.550 --> 00:12:22.510
Where a surgeon actually remotely
operates the robotic arm to be able to

00:12:22.510 --> 00:12:28.220
do a surgery live, either in the same
room or even in a distant location.

00:12:28.220 --> 00:12:33.312
And so that's virtual reality too
because the surgeon only relies on

00:12:33.312 --> 00:12:38.314
the visual stream of the endoscope
that they see in the remote spot,

00:12:38.314 --> 00:12:41.849
which is basically
a virtual reality display.

00:12:41.849 --> 00:12:48.100
Fundamentally VR is the new medium that
enables totally unprecedented experiences.

00:12:48.100 --> 00:12:51.586
And we still have to learn
how to use this medium and

00:12:51.586 --> 00:12:54.907
create new types of
experiences effectively.

00:12:54.907 --> 00:12:59.850
So it's a very exciting area of
a lot of dynamics and development.

00:12:59.850 --> 00:13:02.892
Enhancing Virtual Reality is
also one of the grand challenges

00:13:02.892 --> 00:13:04.947
of The National Academy of Engineering.

00:13:04.947 --> 00:13:07.579
So not just from a content creation
side it's interesting, but

00:13:07.579 --> 00:13:08.905
also from an engineering side.

00:13:08.905 --> 00:13:12.938
This brings together computer vision,
display, human perception,

00:13:12.938 --> 00:13:15.838
computer graphics, and
many other disciplines.

00:13:15.838 --> 00:13:18.620
So you can see that's kind of
in this overview slide here.

00:13:18.620 --> 00:13:20.507
There's just a HoloLens in the middle.

00:13:20.507 --> 00:13:22.720
The HoloLens has so many different parts.

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:27.623
It has custom made chips such as CPU's,
GPU's, central processing units,

00:13:27.623 --> 00:13:32.340
graphics processing units, but
also image processing units.

00:13:32.340 --> 00:13:36.378
We have a whole array of sensors that
do scene understanding, hand tracking,

00:13:36.378 --> 00:13:38.589
positional tracking, and other things.

00:13:38.589 --> 00:13:41.893
We need to understand human perception and
it's limitation so

00:13:41.893 --> 00:13:44.240
we can build effective displays.

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:48.451
And at the same time,
we need to create content, for example,

00:13:48.451 --> 00:13:51.793
using things like cameras or
computer graphics.

00:13:51.793 --> 00:13:56.798
So I just wanna spend one or two minutes
on telling you a little more about

00:13:56.798 --> 00:14:01.988
our research area, or one of them,
to advance virtual reality display.

00:14:01.988 --> 00:14:03.243
So normally a phone or

00:14:03.243 --> 00:14:07.200
a small display inside a head
mounted display behind a lens.

00:14:07.200 --> 00:14:10.680
And your eyes will look at
a magnified view of this image.

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:14.233
This will be simply bigger but
it also gets further away.

00:14:14.233 --> 00:14:17.640
So each eye will basically look at
a different plane floating in space.

00:14:17.640 --> 00:14:19.200
That's how we can think about it.

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:21.121
And that will be at a fixed distance.

00:14:21.121 --> 00:14:25.606
So the focal distance of this display
is gonna be at one, fixed distance, and

00:14:25.606 --> 00:14:28.165
that's not a natural viewing experience.

00:14:28.165 --> 00:14:29.467
If you think about 3D vision.

00:14:29.467 --> 00:14:34.122
And here's just a one-minute
overview of 3D human perception.

00:14:34.122 --> 00:14:36.780
There are many different cues
that the human brain uses to

00:14:36.780 --> 00:14:38.180
interpret depth from a scene.

00:14:39.190 --> 00:14:40.340
So there's vergence,

00:14:40.340 --> 00:14:43.569
that's the relative rotation of
your eyeballs in their sockets.

00:14:43.569 --> 00:14:46.865
It's an oculomotor cue, so a muscular cue.

00:14:46.865 --> 00:14:49.000
This is driven by binocular disparity,

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:51.960
the fact that we see two
different images with our eyes.

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:55.656
And this is easy to create with
virtual reality displays because we

00:14:55.656 --> 00:14:59.561
simply render slightly different
perspectives in front of each eye.

00:14:59.561 --> 00:15:04.190
But here's the challenge, the eye
also focuses it's crystalline lens.

00:15:04.190 --> 00:15:06.738
And that's driven by retinal blur,

00:15:06.738 --> 00:15:10.938
which is similar to a depth of
field effect in the SLR camera.

00:15:10.938 --> 00:15:15.078
This is a cue that we can't easily
reproduce in virtual reality because

00:15:15.078 --> 00:15:19.280
we cannot optically correct
this retinal blur cue easily.

00:15:19.280 --> 00:15:23.162
All these cues are coupled together and
that's what creates a lot of trouble.

00:15:23.162 --> 00:15:26.917
So in the real world as you
look at different objects,

00:15:26.917 --> 00:15:31.767
your eyes will verge towards these
distances, so they will rotate.

00:15:31.767 --> 00:15:35.173
But they will also accommodate, so
they will focus to these distances.

00:15:35.173 --> 00:15:37.989
So all these cues are always
working in harmony together.

00:15:41.168 --> 00:15:44.694
In virtual reality there's mismatch
between these cues, I'll try and

00:15:44.694 --> 00:15:45.861
illustrate that here.

00:15:45.861 --> 00:15:49.360
We can render images so
that you converge at arbitrary distances.

00:15:49.360 --> 00:15:53.708
But the focus of your eyes will always
be linked to that floating plane,

00:15:53.708 --> 00:15:55.600
the magnified image in space.

00:15:55.600 --> 00:15:59.758
So this discrepancy between different cues
that the brain receives is something that

00:15:59.758 --> 00:16:01.570
usually creates.

00:16:01.570 --> 00:16:05.710
Eye stress, visual discomfort nausea and
other undesired effects.

00:16:05.710 --> 00:16:10.660
So, our group does a lot of research in
trying to address this for example and

00:16:10.660 --> 00:16:13.550
here is again an image of
the fixed focused to light

00:16:13.550 --> 00:16:18.480
where we place a phone behind a fixed
distance of the lens, a couple of

00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:23.270
simple ideas that we're addressing
that's focus issue would be to either

00:16:24.580 --> 00:16:28.840
actuate the display by slightly displacing
it inside the head mounted display,

00:16:28.840 --> 00:16:31.250
which changes the magnified
image distance.

00:16:32.340 --> 00:16:35.510
Another way would be to use unconventional
objects that can dynamically

00:16:35.510 --> 00:16:39.480
update the focal power of the lens,
and this is a very effective way to

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:44.870
change the magnified image distance and
accommodation of the human observer.

00:16:44.870 --> 00:16:48.290
In practice, we need to use eye tracking
for our head monitor display so

00:16:48.290 --> 00:16:52.200
we build a very simple prototype that you
can see here, which has a stereoscopic eye

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:57.380
tracker and a motor mounted to
the focus adjustment mechanisms of the.

00:16:57.380 --> 00:16:59.210
Okay, so here's this prototype.

00:16:59.210 --> 00:17:01.950
We can basically trace the motor
very quickly based on the tracked

00:17:01.950 --> 00:17:03.480
eye position of the viewer.

00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:07.590
And with only few millimeters of
mechanical statement we can actually

00:17:07.590 --> 00:17:11.090
change the focus plan of
the display by a large amount of

00:17:11.090 --> 00:17:12.700
the entire range of which you can focus.

00:17:12.700 --> 00:17:17.800
So that's what you can learn about on
the overview of what virtual reality,

00:17:17.800 --> 00:17:20.070
computer vision and imaging is all about.

00:17:20.070 --> 00:17:25.309
These are graphics and all of these topics
including virtual reality this consist.

00:17:25.309 --> 00:17:28.804
Photograph of the Stanford's EE 267 class.

