00:19:34.120 --> 00:19:37.373
And in machine learning
we are going to as we go.

00:19:37.373 --> 00:19:41.645
So, going back to my lab what we've
done for 20 years is critical simulation

00:19:41.645 --> 00:19:45.399
you've seen it in many movies here
are some examples, Davie Jones,

00:19:45.399 --> 00:19:46.776
Harry Porter etc, etc.

00:19:46.776 --> 00:19:51.459
And when doing physical simulation for
movies what we focus on is many many

00:19:51.459 --> 00:19:56.748
degrees of freedom, water, wrinkles and
and cloth waves underwater, smoke that

00:19:56.748 --> 00:20:01.750
sought of thing and you really want
high visual fidelity, very rich details.

00:20:03.972 --> 00:20:06.770
But what that comes from
is computational physics.

00:20:06.770 --> 00:20:08.636
There's equations for the water.

00:20:08.636 --> 00:20:11.070
There's models for the.

00:20:11.070 --> 00:20:13.620
And so we look to all those things
in order to do the simulations.

00:20:13.620 --> 00:20:15.150
But if you look into
computational physics,

00:20:15.150 --> 00:20:19.500
what you find is that your algorithms are
really good for finding a drag on a wing.

00:20:19.500 --> 00:20:24.160
Where the lift kind of wing or
modelling a scram jet engine and

00:20:24.160 --> 00:20:25.740
vaporization of drops.

00:20:25.740 --> 00:20:30.410
They're very very accurate or
faithful to real world data.

00:20:30.410 --> 00:20:32.270
And it's not we want for graphics.

00:20:32.270 --> 00:20:35.700
You want stuff which is sought
of hyper real in that fence.

00:20:35.700 --> 00:20:38.360
So computation of physics
is a motivation for

00:20:38.360 --> 00:20:43.300
what we do but it's not just taking the
equations from computational physics and

00:20:43.300 --> 00:20:45.640
reimplementing them in
a graphics pipeline.

00:20:45.640 --> 00:20:47.510
It's doing something different with them.

00:20:47.510 --> 00:20:50.870
During last 20 years, I've put half of
my papers in computational physics, and

00:20:50.870 --> 00:20:56.840
half of my papers in graphics in my 40
PhD students 30 former and 10 current.

00:20:56.840 --> 00:20:59.526
It really struggled along with me,
to look back and

00:20:59.526 --> 00:21:03.085
forth between those two different
areas and target simulations and

00:21:03.085 --> 00:21:07.536
methodologies towards those areas and
it turns out, it's very, very different.

00:21:07.536 --> 00:21:11.779
You can't pick up stuff from one and
put it in the other and vice versa and

00:21:11.779 --> 00:21:16.167
that's been something that really helped
up lead the field in this area is

00:21:16.167 --> 00:21:18.178
understanding what goes where.

00:21:18.178 --> 00:21:20.463
You're like [INAUDIBLE] is that
an idea for computational physics or

00:21:20.463 --> 00:21:21.267
an idea for graphics?

00:21:21.267 --> 00:21:23.900
Yeah, if something in physics,
does it work in graphics or

00:21:23.900 --> 00:21:26.400
does it not work in graphics,
and vice versa.

00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:30.890
And that's been the essence
of my research program for

00:21:30.890 --> 00:21:32.630
the entire 20 years that I've been in it,

00:21:32.630 --> 00:21:36.470
is just differentiating carefully
between those two areas with each idea.

00:21:37.540 --> 00:21:39.330
No round peg in the square
hole-type stuff.

00:21:39.330 --> 00:21:40.394
You have to avoid that.

00:21:42.683 --> 00:21:47.249
So more recently, say two years ago, I
started realizing there's a third area of

00:21:47.249 --> 00:21:52.120
simulation which is very,
very different from what I had been doing.

00:21:52.120 --> 00:21:56.120
And it took me a while to even
realize that even though I'd spent 20

00:21:56.120 --> 00:21:58.870
years differentiating
the feature film technology from

00:21:58.870 --> 00:22:03.720
the computational physics technology,
putting about 50 papers in each one.

00:22:03.720 --> 00:22:09.550
But it started to sink in a little bit and
the idea was that if somebody went out and

00:22:09.550 --> 00:22:14.620
filmed some or filmed the waves on
the ocean, and you want it to somehow

00:22:14.620 --> 00:22:18.320
use those waves in your simulation
to get better looking water, or

00:22:18.320 --> 00:22:20.790
water that looked like a lake,
or an ocean, or a bathtub, or

00:22:20.790 --> 00:22:26.140
whatever it was, and use the data or
you scanned in some cloth.

00:22:26.140 --> 00:22:27.050
You saw how it moved.

00:22:27.050 --> 00:22:28.070
You wanted to use that.

00:22:28.070 --> 00:22:32.140
Or, for human tissue, if you took a
bodybuilder, scanned in their muscles and

00:22:32.140 --> 00:22:34.680
how they flex, then you can do a
simulation of biomechanics in the muscles.

00:22:34.680 --> 00:22:36.570
You want to be faithful to that.

00:22:36.570 --> 00:22:38.045
How would you actually do that?

00:22:38.045 --> 00:22:41.574
I originally started out doing that with
what would be number two on this slide.

00:22:41.574 --> 00:22:45.279
I took the computational physics approach
to be faithful to real world data.

00:22:45.279 --> 00:22:50.121
I pushed into biomechanics for awhile,
and what they did there, but it turns

00:22:50.121 --> 00:22:55.680
out the biomechanical stuff doesn't
really lend itself well to the data.

00:22:55.680 --> 00:23:00.130
And so, it took a while but we start to
realize it's really is a third paradigm

00:23:00.130 --> 00:23:02.750
for a physical simulation
which is popping up.

00:23:02.750 --> 00:23:05.810
And just isn't true for computational
physics as far as like smoke water,

00:23:05.810 --> 00:23:10.110
fire and human bodies, it's also true for
rendering in other areas.

00:23:10.110 --> 00:23:15.800
Think of that not as just simple
simulation but just simulation in general.

00:23:15.800 --> 00:23:18.510
Anytime you're synthesising
the real world,

00:23:18.510 --> 00:23:20.970
you want to think about
different ways of doing it.

00:23:20.970 --> 00:23:23.760
You're either trying to be faithful
to the real world, which would be

00:23:23.760 --> 00:23:26.900
the computational physics or you're
trying to have something really cool,

00:23:26.900 --> 00:23:31.880
like a game or a movie, that would be
the feature film type stuff on this slide.

00:23:31.880 --> 00:23:35.764
Or you really want it to sort of have
the look and feel of the real data.

00:23:37.881 --> 00:23:41.614
So, we start looking more
carefully into data statistics and

00:23:41.614 --> 00:23:44.430
machine learning and
the first thing is data.

00:23:44.430 --> 00:23:45.980
Data is what's really driving this.

00:23:45.980 --> 00:23:47.480
There's more and more data everywhere.

00:23:47.480 --> 00:23:50.920
Wherever you look, people are collecting
data, storing data in Internet,

00:23:50.920 --> 00:23:52.970
pushing data around and as more and

00:23:52.970 --> 00:23:56.250
more data start showing up, they get
better ways of collecting data and

00:23:56.250 --> 00:23:59.400
the better ways you get of collecting
data, the more and more data you get.

00:24:01.540 --> 00:24:03.890
Statistics and machine learning
are obviously the areas that work

00:24:03.890 --> 00:24:04.830
with the data.

00:24:04.830 --> 00:24:07.500
Actually, statistics, you would think
just the statisticians work with data,

00:24:07.500 --> 00:24:08.787
that's been their job for a long time.

00:24:08.787 --> 00:24:13.375
The thing is statisticians never had that
much data, so in theoretical statistics,

00:24:13.375 --> 00:24:17.222
it was really a way to talking about
dealing with small amounts of data.

00:24:17.222 --> 00:24:21.770
Even today, if you look at political
polls, they poll 1,000 people and

00:24:21.770 --> 00:24:27.550
that's supposed to represent what tens or
hundreds of millions of people would do.

00:24:27.550 --> 00:24:29.550
Well, why not poll 100,000 people,
or a million people?

00:24:29.550 --> 00:24:31.640
That would be much better.

00:24:31.640 --> 00:24:32.940
But statisticians worked very,

00:24:32.940 --> 00:24:37.550
very hard at using tiny amounts of
data to represent large populations.

00:24:37.550 --> 00:24:38.530
But the world's changing.

00:24:38.530 --> 00:24:41.310
Now we have large amounts of data,
we can get a lot more data and

00:24:41.310 --> 00:24:44.645
they're not really taking advantage
of it at the speed one would expect.

00:24:44.645 --> 00:24:48.600
If you look at math departments,
going back historically.

00:24:49.900 --> 00:24:53.280
Once computers start taking off, these
new mathematicians started showing up.

00:24:53.280 --> 00:24:55.760
They weren't theoretical anymore,
they were applied mathematicians,

00:24:55.760 --> 00:24:59.020
they were numerical analysts,
computational physics type people.

00:24:59.020 --> 00:25:02.668
And it was sort of a new way of thinking
about the world through the computer,

00:25:02.668 --> 00:25:06.430
as far as mathematics went, as opposed to
the way it was done through pencil and

00:25:06.430 --> 00:25:07.349
paper in the past.

00:25:07.349 --> 00:25:11.133
And once you had the computer,
you had things like numerical algebra,

00:25:11.133 --> 00:25:14.287
numerical optimization,
numerical ODEs, and PDEs, and

00:25:14.287 --> 00:25:17.220
new mathematics had to spring out of this.

00:25:17.220 --> 00:25:20.057
Similarly, as we add all this data and
the statistics that they never had,

00:25:20.057 --> 00:25:22.230
you're gonna need a new way
of dealing with statistics.

00:25:22.230 --> 00:25:23.912
Then what's happened,

00:25:23.912 --> 00:25:28.315
which is kind of neat from my
standpoint as a computer scientist,

00:25:28.315 --> 00:25:34.196
is that people in my department,
Daphne Koller, Andrew Sebastian Thrun.

00:25:34.196 --> 00:25:37.995
They sort of picked up the ball on this
and started taking all this data and

00:25:37.995 --> 00:25:41.859
the computers and everything else In
developing this new area which we now

00:25:41.859 --> 00:25:43.500
refer to as machine learning.

00:25:43.500 --> 00:25:48.200
But machine learning people are really the
next generation of applied mathematicians.

00:25:48.200 --> 00:25:51.402
They're coming out of statistics
departments because of the data similar to

00:25:51.402 --> 00:25:54.654
the way the applied mathematicians come
out of the math departments because of

00:25:54.654 --> 00:25:56.342
the computers, going back in the past.

00:25:56.342 --> 00:25:58.963
So it's really drawn me
towards machine learning,

00:25:58.963 --> 00:26:02.310
which is part the artificial
intelligence groups.

00:26:02.310 --> 00:26:05.251
In fact on Tuesday, I formally
joined their AI lab at Stanford.

00:26:08.071 --> 00:26:12.825
So the new part for me, that machine
learning people have been really knocking

00:26:12.825 --> 00:26:16.540
the ball out of the part for
five, ten years now.

00:26:16.540 --> 00:26:19.680
And the new part for me is looking at all
this stuff through the lens of physics,

00:26:19.680 --> 00:26:20.783
because I do simulations.

00:26:20.783 --> 00:26:24.245
And so when look to things similar
to physics as opposed to statistics,

00:26:24.245 --> 00:26:25.990
things are a little bit different.

00:26:28.100 --> 00:26:30.690
Here's a good example,
suppose I had ten balls.

00:26:30.690 --> 00:26:35.300
And I took each ball and I dropped it
from shoulder height to the ground.

00:26:35.300 --> 00:26:37.589
And I measured how long it
took to go to the ground and

00:26:37.589 --> 00:26:39.941
I tried to measure
the acceleration due to gravity.

00:26:39.941 --> 00:26:45.140
And suppose 9 balls fell with speed 9.8,
then 1 ball hovered in mid-air.

00:26:45.140 --> 00:26:51.316
A statistician might say, well, okay,
it's about 9.6 gravity on average.

00:26:51.316 --> 00:26:53.780
Or they might say, no, no, that one's
an outlier, let's get rid of it,

00:26:53.780 --> 00:26:54.500
it's about 9.8.

00:26:54.500 --> 00:26:58.416
A physicist might say, holy crap,
that ball's floating in mid-air,

00:26:58.416 --> 00:26:59.475
what's going on?

00:26:59.475 --> 00:27:02.107
[LAUGH] So it's a different way
of thinking about the data,

00:27:02.107 --> 00:27:05.710
which is like looking at what's actually
happening with the physics as opposed

00:27:05.710 --> 00:27:08.990
to just statistically kind of waving
your hands and saying it's noise,

00:27:08.990 --> 00:27:10.517
it's outliers, this and that.

00:27:10.517 --> 00:27:13.210
It's looking at the data
a little bit differently.

00:27:13.210 --> 00:27:14.160
A good example is incompressibility.

00:27:14.160 --> 00:27:18.107
For example, if I have a bunch of data and
it doesn't represent incompressible flow,

00:27:18.107 --> 00:27:21.990
I know that data needs to go through
some filter to make it incompressible.

00:27:21.990 --> 00:27:24.860
As opposed to just saying this
noise outlier is your other thing.

00:27:24.860 --> 00:27:27.940
These are often called priors
in machine learning, and

00:27:27.940 --> 00:27:29.540
many people use these priors.

00:27:29.540 --> 00:27:32.580
But it's sort of an advantage that you
have if you come from physics to look at

00:27:32.580 --> 00:27:34.920
it, and it's something we're
trying to do in our group.

00:27:34.920 --> 00:27:38.222
So again, just to summarize
before I show you a few examples,

00:27:38.222 --> 00:27:42.223
the goal of our research these days is,
and it's underline at the bottom,

00:27:42.223 --> 00:27:45.272
is to design new numerical
algorithms to better allow for

00:27:45.272 --> 00:27:49.390
the incorporation of data into
the simulations themselves.

00:27:49.390 --> 00:27:52.230
Make things super real,
not just a cloth simulation for a movie,

00:27:52.230 --> 00:27:54.500
which is really nice, but super real.

00:27:54.500 --> 00:27:55.730
Pulled from real world cloth.

00:27:57.330 --> 00:28:01.150
So some projects, the first project
that got us into it was face simulation.

00:28:01.150 --> 00:28:04.483
And I've been at Stanford for 17 years,

00:28:04.483 --> 00:28:08.740
full professor,
I've been here for a while.

00:28:08.740 --> 00:28:12.032
So not a lot of things are really
over the top impressive.

00:28:12.032 --> 00:28:15.483
I've seen all the impressive stuff for
17 years, and

00:28:15.483 --> 00:28:20.490
you kind of get a little bit callous, or
a little bit more relaxed as time goes on.

00:28:20.490 --> 00:28:21.142
But this idea really hit me.

00:28:21.142 --> 00:28:24.470
It was the Wednesday after Thanksgiving.

00:28:24.470 --> 00:28:28.970
And we had this idea for
muscle simulation for the faces.

00:28:28.970 --> 00:28:31.618
And my grad student took the idea and
implemented it.

00:28:31.618 --> 00:28:34.586
And by that evening, he showed me
a simulation with a face that was better

00:28:34.586 --> 00:28:37.130
than anything that I'd ever seen before,
by a quantum leap.

00:28:37.130 --> 00:28:43.640
And the big thing we did was we took
a bunch of data of faces moving around.

00:28:43.640 --> 00:28:47.430
And we designed a simulation system
that could actually target that data.

00:28:47.430 --> 00:28:51.266
So in the past when we did simulations,
you flexed muscles, you moved them around,

00:28:51.266 --> 00:28:51.816
whatever.

00:28:51.816 --> 00:28:55.410
They're biomechanically accurate,
physically accurate.

00:28:55.410 --> 00:28:59.109
But what we did here is we learned
how to target the actual data, and

00:28:59.109 --> 00:29:00.970
it really made a big difference.

00:29:00.970 --> 00:29:03.860
In fact we already used it
in King Kong Skull Island.

00:29:05.098 --> 00:29:07.300
We had the idea,
we wrote a paper in less than two months.

00:29:07.300 --> 00:29:10.740
It was implemented in less than 12 hours,
paper in less than 2 months and

00:29:10.740 --> 00:29:13.760
it went into movie production
literally right after that.

00:29:13.760 --> 00:29:18.160
Then within less than one year we finished
an entire movie with it from idea

00:29:18.160 --> 00:29:22.090
conception to completion, and it really
made a huge difference in the movie.

00:29:23.620 --> 00:29:25.680
We've been building models for
a long time.

00:29:25.680 --> 00:29:30.310
This is maybe a 15-year-old picture now of
one of my graduate students that we flew

00:29:30.310 --> 00:29:35.420
up to Canada and covered in latex and
built some high resolution muscle models

00:29:35.420 --> 00:29:39.220
using the old Matrix technology from
the Matrix films if you remember that.

00:29:39.220 --> 00:29:42.890
These days it's easier, you can just
scan in a statue with the scanners.

00:29:42.890 --> 00:29:45.709
We have much better
graphic technology now and

00:29:45.709 --> 00:29:48.172
build a triangle face model out of that.

00:29:48.172 --> 00:29:52.429
We outfit these with finite element
models, biomechanically accurate muscles,

00:29:52.429 --> 00:29:54.080
everything you would imagine.

00:29:55.110 --> 00:29:59.730
And we use cameras and computer vision
in order to track what faces do.

00:30:01.340 --> 00:30:06.490
In the end then, you have some model for
how the face moves.

00:30:06.490 --> 00:30:10.688
Typically using a lot of this you can
build an exterior shell model which is on

00:30:10.688 --> 00:30:13.520
the left,
which is just triangles moving around.

00:30:13.520 --> 00:30:16.800
But using some of our new technology,
you can take a skull and

00:30:16.800 --> 00:30:18.910
put it inside that face on the left.

00:30:18.910 --> 00:30:21.760
This is what the right picture is,
there's a skull underneath.

00:30:21.760 --> 00:30:25.680
And put muscles underneath the skull and
flex all the muscles in order to try and

00:30:25.680 --> 00:30:28.660
make the face deform in order
to match the thing on the left.

00:30:28.660 --> 00:30:32.314
So on the left is just
a sculpt made by a modeler.

00:30:32.314 --> 00:30:36.150
He just went in and used some animation
software to move around all the triangles,

00:30:36.150 --> 00:30:38.200
get a really nice looking face.

00:30:38.200 --> 00:30:41.133
On the right is a muscle simulation
which is supposed to mimic that,

00:30:41.133 --> 00:30:44.020
and if you look at the two,
they're very similar, very similar.

00:30:44.020 --> 00:30:46.320
But there's some small differences.

00:30:46.320 --> 00:30:47.886
For example, look at the lips on the left.

00:30:47.886 --> 00:30:51.280
They're a little bit washed out,
a little too smooth, and on the right,

00:30:51.280 --> 00:30:52.960
they're a little more vivid.

00:30:52.960 --> 00:30:56.460
And we captured that bit more vivid
because we used the simulation

00:30:56.460 --> 00:30:57.790
stuff underneath.

00:30:57.790 --> 00:30:59.941
I might say, okay it's a small change,
why does it even matter?

00:30:59.941 --> 00:31:04.310
The reason it matters is the sculpt on
the left was supposed to be perfect.

00:31:04.310 --> 00:31:06.900
An artist made this,
it was gold standard, and

00:31:06.900 --> 00:31:11.090
it was something we used in
the FX industry as a perfect.

00:31:11.090 --> 00:31:14.062
And then we ran one muscle simulation
on it one time just to check it out and

00:31:14.062 --> 00:31:15.100
try to target that data.

00:31:15.100 --> 00:31:18.440
And we were like, wow,
the lips could be better.

00:31:18.440 --> 00:31:21.990
We actually don't have a perfect thing,
it's actually much improved.

00:31:21.990 --> 00:31:25.324
One thing we struggle with in the effects
industry with humans is we make them more

00:31:25.324 --> 00:31:25.970
and more real.

00:31:25.970 --> 00:31:29.655
There's something called the uncanny
valley where all of a sudden if something

00:31:29.655 --> 00:31:32.580
gets too real it's disturbing
if it's not completely real.

00:31:32.580 --> 00:31:36.370
There's a gap there between things
getting better and getting all the way.

00:31:36.370 --> 00:31:38.328
And so when you get to this gap,
you have to be very careful.

00:31:38.328 --> 00:31:42.407
You typically pull back and make the halt
green or change things a little bit so

00:31:42.407 --> 00:31:45.550
it feels less real, so you can accept it.

00:31:45.550 --> 00:31:49.870
In fact, take a completely real human and
change it just a little bit, for

00:31:49.870 --> 00:31:52.540
example, put it in a casket.

00:31:52.540 --> 00:31:53.800
That's pretty scary, right?

00:31:53.800 --> 00:31:56.060
Or a zombie, you think about,
that's kind of scary.

00:31:56.060 --> 00:31:59.590
So these things which are almost real but
not real, they're very disturbing.

00:31:59.590 --> 00:32:01.000
And they don't work well in movies, so

00:32:01.000 --> 00:32:03.105
we're trying to get by
this uncanny valley.

00:32:03.105 --> 00:32:05.280
If we knew what was wrong,
we'd just fix it.

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:06.960
This is an example of when
we know what's wrong.

00:32:06.960 --> 00:32:09.910
The thing on the left we thought was
perfect, but it turns out the lips

00:32:09.910 --> 00:32:13.300
could be better, we learned that from
the simulation, so we can make it better.

00:32:13.300 --> 00:32:17.096
And this is the main thing we want to
address in the movie industry right now

00:32:17.096 --> 00:32:20.280
using this technology,
which is can we make the faces real and

00:32:20.280 --> 00:32:22.874
get through the uncanny
valley to the other side?

00:32:27.813 --> 00:32:31.190
Here's a video that shows some of
the other benefits of this though.

00:32:31.190 --> 00:32:34.360
In the upper left is a bunch
of sculpts done by an artist.

00:32:34.360 --> 00:32:36.580
Just a triangle mesh moving around.

00:32:36.580 --> 00:32:40.740
In the middle is the result of the muscle
simulation targeting in every frame

00:32:40.740 --> 00:32:42.570
just like in the last slide.

00:32:42.570 --> 00:32:44.939
In the far right,
you can see the skull and

00:32:44.939 --> 00:32:48.801
some of the muscles moving around
underneath the middle to show what

00:32:48.801 --> 00:32:52.341
the skull muscles had to do in
order to target the simulation.

00:32:52.341 --> 00:32:55.635
What’s nice here is, if I take
the far left model which is a bunch

00:32:55.635 --> 00:32:59.296
of triangles and I build a skull for
it on the far upper right in muscles,

00:32:59.296 --> 00:33:01.798
and I can figure out how
to target the animation,

00:33:01.798 --> 00:33:06.910
then I have some knowledge of how things
are moving which aren't just triangles.

00:33:06.910 --> 00:33:11.225
So if I take that Yoda statue we scanned
in on the lower left and I fit a skull and

00:33:11.225 --> 00:33:15.135
muscles to that, then I can take
the upper right skull and muscles and

00:33:15.135 --> 00:33:19.478
map that one to one to the lower right
skull and muscles and move them around.

00:33:19.478 --> 00:33:24.560
And I can use it to animate the scanned
in Yoda mesh without any artists' help or

00:33:24.560 --> 00:33:27.410
any animation or any packages or anything.

00:33:27.410 --> 00:33:31.130
It's literally just a muscle simulation
going forward, making Yoda do stuff.

00:33:31.130 --> 00:33:33.620
Existing technology on the upper left,
map them to

00:33:34.800 --> 00:33:38.190
the triangles on the lower left Yoda,
and try to push things around, but

00:33:38.190 --> 00:33:41.340
since there's a fat lip on the guy in the
upper left compared to Yoda's very thin

00:33:41.340 --> 00:33:45.900
lips, you'd actually see a fat lip moving
around on the Yoda, it wouldn't look good.

00:33:45.900 --> 00:33:49.167
There's not a good one to one
correspondence between the facial

00:33:49.167 --> 00:33:51.842
geometries of the Yoda and
the guy in the upper left.

00:33:51.842 --> 00:33:55.254
But the muscles underneath have a fairly
good one to one correspondence,

00:33:55.254 --> 00:33:58.170
because we're all actuating
things the same way.

00:33:58.170 --> 00:34:02.210
And if I actuate a muscle or open my
jaw or smile or do something like that,

00:34:02.210 --> 00:34:03.520
that looks different in every person.

00:34:03.520 --> 00:34:06.875
But everybody's basically trying to
activate the muscles in the same way.

00:34:06.875 --> 00:34:09.010
And so you get a nice one-to-one mapping.

00:34:09.010 --> 00:34:11.714
It's much better than principle component
analysis or any of the other methods,

00:34:11.714 --> 00:34:13.057
because it's more physically driven.

00:34:19.060 --> 00:34:21.370
So another project I'm
gonna talk about is cloth.

00:34:21.370 --> 00:34:22.680
This is one of our very big ones.

00:34:22.680 --> 00:34:25.191
We've been doing cloth simulation
in the lab for a long time.

00:34:25.191 --> 00:34:27.217
These are actually ten-year-old
pictures of cloth and

00:34:27.217 --> 00:34:28.830
all the details we've
been able to collect.

00:34:30.510 --> 00:34:32.180
Cloth's used a lot in movies.

00:34:32.180 --> 00:34:34.710
Frozen's like a cartoon
example of using cloth,

00:34:34.710 --> 00:34:38.860
and Yoda in Star Wars is a good example,
his robe, of a more realistic version.

00:34:39.880 --> 00:34:41.460
The thing is, the real world has much,

00:34:41.460 --> 00:34:44.310
much better cloth than
what we see in movies.

00:34:44.310 --> 00:34:48.250
If I go into a movie theater and shut out
all the lights and turn on the sound and

00:34:48.250 --> 00:34:52.100
tell you a story, and show you the cloth
on Yoda, you might say, that looks real.

00:34:52.100 --> 00:34:53.870
That looks like real cloth to me.

00:34:53.870 --> 00:34:55.550
But you also think Yoda is real.

00:34:55.550 --> 00:34:57.440
And Yoda's not real obviously.

00:34:57.440 --> 00:35:00.840
But somewhere during the movie where
it was dark with the lights out, and

00:35:00.840 --> 00:35:02.840
you really wanted to
believe your suspension and

00:35:02.840 --> 00:35:05.540
disbelief, you actually start
thinking Yoda was real.

00:35:05.540 --> 00:35:06.710
So it's very easy for

00:35:06.710 --> 00:35:09.500
me to make a pretty crummy piece
of cloth appear real to you too.

00:35:09.500 --> 00:35:10.450
You want it to be real.

00:35:10.450 --> 00:35:12.494
But you want Yoda to be real.

00:35:12.494 --> 00:35:14.230
We have in the movie theater.

00:35:14.230 --> 00:35:17.444
But if I go into e-commerce or something
and wanna do some cloth on your body,

00:35:17.444 --> 00:35:20.212
you're not gonna wanna see something
that's supposedly real and

00:35:20.212 --> 00:35:21.660
doesn't really fit.

00:35:21.660 --> 00:35:23.687
You return it to the store
after you order it.

00:35:23.687 --> 00:35:26.859
So there's really a gap between
the movie cloth where you're willing to

00:35:26.859 --> 00:35:29.820
accept the cloth,
where you want the cloth to seem real.

00:35:29.820 --> 00:35:32.390
And the cloth that you actually
would have in e-commerce,

00:35:32.390 --> 00:35:33.720
to show how it's fitting on your body.

00:35:33.720 --> 00:35:36.290
Something you could buy reliably online,

00:35:36.290 --> 00:35:39.660
know how it's gonna fit, ship to your
house where it had to fit that way and

00:35:39.660 --> 00:35:41.460
be happy with it and
not return it to the store.

00:35:43.330 --> 00:35:46.040
So this is another place
where we wanna use this data.

00:35:46.040 --> 00:35:49.130
We wanna take real world cloth,
scan it all in,

00:35:49.130 --> 00:35:50.530
get all the data from how it looks.

00:35:50.530 --> 00:35:53.850
How it folds, how it behaves, and
stuffed it into the simulations so

00:35:53.850 --> 00:35:55.240
we can do simulations of it.

00:35:55.240 --> 00:35:59.210
We can learn from it and have it
actually represent what would happen.

00:35:59.210 --> 00:36:04.000
So you could scan a body model of
yourself, move your body around, have some

00:36:04.000 --> 00:36:07.530
virtual cloth on you that moves and
folds and acts just like the real cloth.

00:36:07.530 --> 00:36:11.950
Moreover, if I can actually mimic the real
world cloth and get the data into my

00:36:11.950 --> 00:36:15.691
simulations, I can go back to Yoda and
make a robe on him which is so

00:36:15.691 --> 00:36:19.174
super real that you couldn't
even make it in the real world.

00:36:19.174 --> 00:36:23.170
And it behaves the way the real world does
but it's even better than the real world.

00:36:23.170 --> 00:36:26.110
And that's what we really want in
the movies is something which is hyper

00:36:26.110 --> 00:36:27.049
real and even better.

00:36:27.049 --> 00:36:29.260
But if we can't do the real world cloth,
it's gonna be tough.

00:36:30.450 --> 00:36:34.521
So what we're doing in the cloth right
now is it's the very early stages, but

00:36:34.521 --> 00:36:37.103
we're already learning
a whole bunch about it.

00:36:37.103 --> 00:36:41.004
For example, if you look at the figure on
the right here, if you have a curve and

00:36:41.004 --> 00:36:44.731
the curve represents a scan of a real
world cloth, there's a lot of folds,

00:36:44.731 --> 00:36:48.044
this is a black curve and I down
sample it to something I can simulate

00:36:48.044 --> 00:36:50.940
on the computer which
would be the blue curve.

00:36:50.940 --> 00:36:52.952
Notice that she has how
the distance gets shorter.

00:36:52.952 --> 00:36:55.540
The lengths of the black line
between two of the blue points is

00:36:55.540 --> 00:36:58.810
longer than the lengths of the blue lines,
I'm compressing all the edges.

00:36:59.850 --> 00:37:04.386
And so if you build a database doing this
for machine learning or anything else,

00:37:04.386 --> 00:37:08.989
you scan a bunch of clothe express it down
and make a function from the coarse cloth

00:37:08.989 --> 00:37:13.729
to the fine cloth and then you simulate
your cloth, what's gonna happen is you're

00:37:13.729 --> 00:37:17.550
simulating the coarse cloth and
you can't look up the fine cloth.

00:37:17.550 --> 00:37:20.768
Let me explain that with
a projection of an example.

00:37:20.768 --> 00:37:22.717
Look at the checkerboard on the far left.

00:37:22.717 --> 00:37:26.229
If you have a piece of paper and
it's orthogonal to the camera, and

00:37:26.229 --> 00:37:29.253
you project a square,
it's going to show up as a square.

00:37:29.253 --> 00:37:33.610
But if you bend that thing away from the
camera, it's gonna stretch the square out.

00:37:33.610 --> 00:37:36.909
So in order to make it actually
appear like a square on a camera,

00:37:36.909 --> 00:37:41.142
as they do in this paper up in 2016, you
have to take the edges of the square and

00:37:41.142 --> 00:37:42.792
compress if the paper is bent.

00:37:42.792 --> 00:37:46.355
And so you have to learn how to compress
things when they're real length in

00:37:46.355 --> 00:37:47.220
the projection.

00:37:47.220 --> 00:37:50.920
I did a very good job with that so
we need to do the same thing in cloth.

00:37:50.920 --> 00:37:52.527
If you look at the upper right here,

00:37:52.527 --> 00:37:56.009
if the resh shape of the piece of cloth is
long and then you look at the line below

00:37:56.009 --> 00:37:59.404
it and you compress it, it wrinkles,
that's what happens to real cloth.

00:37:59.404 --> 00:38:03.512
And in our simulation, if we only have
one edge there, when we compress it, so

00:38:03.512 --> 00:38:07.685
if you look at the third figure down the
simulation edge and when we compress it,

00:38:07.685 --> 00:38:09.020
it just needs to shrink.

00:38:09.020 --> 00:38:11.335
It can't bend,
because it's a single license,

00:38:11.335 --> 00:38:14.473
we don't have the details actually
to bend it, but we want to be able

00:38:14.473 --> 00:38:18.242
to take the real world data of the bending
and wiggling and project it onto there.

00:38:18.242 --> 00:38:21.592
So we needed to actually shrink, so when
we project the wiggling back on there,

00:38:21.592 --> 00:38:23.530
we get the right thing.

00:38:23.530 --> 00:38:27.255
We can't just shrink the cloth by relaxing
all the springs by elements connect it,

00:38:27.255 --> 00:38:29.650
cuz then it also stretches too much,
and so

00:38:29.650 --> 00:38:33.350
what you really need is in set up forcing
edge length on your simulator cloth to be

00:38:33.350 --> 00:38:35.900
equal to the ref length,
you need to constrain them so

00:38:35.900 --> 00:38:40.250
they can't get bigger than the ref length
and sag like the lower right picture.

00:38:40.250 --> 00:38:42.340
But you want them to shrink
left from their edge length,

00:38:42.340 --> 00:38:44.460
because you want them to be compressing,

00:38:44.460 --> 00:38:47.520
where you're gonna add the real-world
data from the wrinkles layer.

00:38:47.520 --> 00:38:50.987
No cloth techniques do that, so
we had to go out and design a new,

00:38:50.987 --> 00:38:54.137
numerical method for
simulating cloth, which allows for

00:38:54.137 --> 00:38:57.949
edge lengths to be constrained with
an upper bound but no lower bound.

00:38:57.949 --> 00:39:02.160
And so, if you look at the lower right,
that's an example of using

00:39:02.160 --> 00:39:05.938
our new Inequality Cloth,
with inequality constraints.

00:39:05.938 --> 00:39:09.675
And if you look at the left picture,
that's using the equality cloth and

00:39:09.675 --> 00:39:13.105
what you can see is when it gets
really detailed in the folds there,

00:39:13.105 --> 00:39:16.353
the equality cloth has much bigger folds,
some buckling, and

00:39:16.353 --> 00:39:20.610
some resistance to the fine details and
wrinkles you'd normally expect.

00:39:20.610 --> 00:39:24.220
Whereas, the equality cloth actually
starts doing the right thing.

00:39:24.220 --> 00:39:26.200
They can start folding
a little bit better.

00:39:26.200 --> 00:39:27.060
Again, it's subtle.

00:39:27.060 --> 00:39:28.440
And there's no data here.

00:39:28.440 --> 00:39:30.949
But it is in the real world,
you'd have many, many more wrinkles,

00:39:30.949 --> 00:39:32.634
which would be superimposing
on this cloth.

00:39:32.634 --> 00:39:35.905
And the cloth's already buckling doing the
wrong thing cuz you're enforcing the edge

00:39:35.905 --> 00:39:37.650
length constraints to be equal.

00:39:37.650 --> 00:39:40.375
Which is incorrect for
a compressed version of cloth,

00:39:40.375 --> 00:39:44.183
then you get this picture on the left and
you can't put the data on it anyhow.

00:39:44.183 --> 00:39:46.145
Think about it like you
have a sheet of paper and

00:39:46.145 --> 00:39:49.520
you compress the paper a little bit and
you want to get some wrinkles.

00:39:49.520 --> 00:39:52.210
Well, if you didn't compress the paper,
if it stayed it's normal size, just added

00:39:52.210 --> 00:39:55.900
wrinkles to it, it'd magically be getting
longer, and adding geodesic distance.

00:39:55.900 --> 00:39:56.850
And that's something you don't want.

00:39:56.850 --> 00:39:58.460
You want the cloth
artificially gaining wrinkles.

00:39:58.460 --> 00:40:00.160
And that's what everyone does now.

00:40:00.160 --> 00:40:02.110
You take Equality Cloth and
add wrinkles to it, so

00:40:02.110 --> 00:40:06.550
they're actually increasing its surface
area, which looks really bad and strange.

00:40:06.550 --> 00:40:10.382
It's like your cloth is growing
wrinkles out of nowhere.

00:40:10.382 --> 00:40:13.210
So, this is where a pipeline
is gonna look like for cloth.

00:40:13.210 --> 00:40:14.910
Basically, if you start on the far right,

00:40:14.910 --> 00:40:18.620
the fine result is scanned
in data from the real world.

00:40:18.620 --> 00:40:23.450
Then you compress it,
which would be the blue bubble,

00:40:23.450 --> 00:40:25.830
second from right, and
you get the coarse result.

00:40:25.830 --> 00:40:27.870
Which are the compress cloths, and

00:40:27.870 --> 00:40:29.910
those would be things you'd
store on the computer.

00:40:29.910 --> 00:40:32.900
Now going to the far left,
if your inequality cloth simulations,

00:40:32.900 --> 00:40:34.920
which will be sort of coarse.

00:40:34.920 --> 00:40:37.604
And you look them up in your
database of course results, and

00:40:37.604 --> 00:40:41.257
then use your machine learning functions
that you've learned or to find the fine

00:40:41.257 --> 00:40:45.036
results from the real world that map to
that, and stuff those two things together.

00:40:45.036 --> 00:40:47.998
The idea is you wanna hybridize
scanning of real world cloth

00:40:47.998 --> 00:40:51.089
with the best simulations we
can do to get ultra-real cloth.

00:40:53.342 --> 00:40:55.540
I wanna mention the bodies for
like one second here.

00:40:55.540 --> 00:40:58.099
We're putting body,
mouth together with muscles.

00:40:58.099 --> 00:40:59.850
We're scanning in weight lifters.

00:40:59.850 --> 00:41:02.100
This is Michael Black's work from Europe.

00:41:02.100 --> 00:41:04.400
We're scanning in people
to learn about the muscles.

00:41:04.400 --> 00:41:07.322
We're trying to add muscles to the body,
because you want to really

00:41:07.322 --> 00:41:10.461
learn how the body works under the cloth,
before you do the cloth on top.

00:41:10.461 --> 00:41:14.629
The idea here is you scan in a really lean
body build where the muscles work first,

00:41:14.629 --> 00:41:16.897
and then shrink the muscles
down to normal,

00:41:16.897 --> 00:41:19.312
it adds that on top to
get a more normal person.

00:41:19.312 --> 00:41:21.480
So trees are the last
topic I want to address.

00:41:22.830 --> 00:41:25.479
And for trees,
a tree is really a multi-body system.

00:41:25.479 --> 00:41:27.176
You can think of a tree as continuous,

00:41:27.176 --> 00:41:30.200
with maybe the leaves as having
angles there, but as a tree bends and

00:41:30.200 --> 00:41:33.520
moves around, you can think of
it as a bunch of cylinders.

00:41:33.520 --> 00:41:36.380
So multi-body systems are commonly done,
we've done a lot of those in our lab.

00:41:36.380 --> 00:41:39.317
For example, Davy Jones tentacles or
something, that we have done.

00:41:39.317 --> 00:41:43.849
We can model tanks, and skeletons, and all
kinds of things, as multi-body systems.

00:41:43.849 --> 00:41:46.460
The problem with a tree,
this tree in particular, which I

00:41:46.460 --> 00:41:50.710
see on my way to Stanford every day on
my bike, is that that's a lot of bodies.

00:41:50.710 --> 00:41:54.353
All those leaves, all those branches,
put a little cylinder for everything.

00:41:54.353 --> 00:41:59.020
Not to mention that's not just a bunch
of cylinders, that's an amazing

00:41:59.020 --> 00:42:03.840
configuration of geometry, but
I have to bend and move and deform.

00:42:03.840 --> 00:42:07.550
It's impossible to do it with a multi-body
system that's way too complex.

00:42:07.550 --> 00:42:12.463
We worked for a long time trying to
build something especially for that.

00:42:12.463 --> 00:42:15.781
In fact we made this specialized approach
which would allow us to do something

00:42:15.781 --> 00:42:17.120
really big.

00:42:17.120 --> 00:42:19.912
Again, the idea was driven
from real world data.

00:42:19.912 --> 00:42:23.670
We wanna actually take a tree model like
this, scan the tree in, build the tree,

00:42:23.670 --> 00:42:26.600
simulate the tree, simulate the wind
hitting the tree and everything else.

00:42:26.600 --> 00:42:29.091
We used the real world data to drive it,
so it looks ultra real.

00:42:31.170 --> 00:42:34.420
So building a simulation system for
this was impossible, and

00:42:34.420 --> 00:42:36.617
we had to do some
research just to do that.

00:42:36.617 --> 00:42:39.283
The neat idea we had in that
research was people often and

00:42:39.283 --> 00:42:40.816
all these robotic mechanisms,

00:42:40.816 --> 00:42:45.250
build these approximations to the exact
solutions to multi-body problems.

00:42:45.250 --> 00:42:46.860
We invert that a little bit.

00:42:46.860 --> 00:42:50.930
We actually made an approximate problem,
and found the exact solution for

00:42:50.930 --> 00:42:52.660
the approximate problem.

00:42:52.660 --> 00:42:56.820
It's got similar errors in many ways,
but because it was an exact solution,

00:42:56.820 --> 00:43:01.050
we could solve it without any of
the stiff issues in numerical OAEs.

00:43:01.050 --> 00:43:04.100
We got rid of stiffness and
time steps to billing and everything else.

00:43:04.100 --> 00:43:06.550
So we could actually simulate something
with many, many, many bodies.

00:43:06.550 --> 00:43:08.190
The simulation was wrong.

00:43:08.190 --> 00:43:10.050
it would never converge
to the right answer,

00:43:10.050 --> 00:43:13.200
cuz we made approximations
in the equations themselves.

00:43:13.200 --> 00:43:16.170
But if you go back to people who
approximate the exact equations,

00:43:16.170 --> 00:43:17.640
they never get there either.

00:43:17.640 --> 00:43:20.406
And so you can bound the errors,
that's not so bad.

00:43:20.406 --> 00:43:24.130
We used this to simulate a whole
bunch of really detailed trees.

00:43:24.130 --> 00:43:27.931
This shows you some geometries we
simulated, but we hated all these trees.

00:43:27.931 --> 00:43:30.238
We used the best systems we
could to build these trees,

00:43:30.238 --> 00:43:33.498
but we never were able to simulate them
to our liking because of the fact that we

00:43:33.498 --> 00:43:34.928
just didn't like the geometry.

00:43:34.928 --> 00:43:39.008
We really wanna simulate that tree,
and so we ran into a problem where we

00:43:39.008 --> 00:43:43.497
couldn't synthesize in graphics the kind
of tree that we wanted to simulate,

00:43:43.497 --> 00:43:46.642
we wanted real world data once again for
simulations.

00:43:46.642 --> 00:43:49.040
And this time we're having trouble
getting the real world data.

00:43:50.700 --> 00:43:53.935
So we went out and tried to use 3D
Computer vision to reconstruct the tree.

00:43:53.935 --> 00:43:57.520
Fact we took drone and cameras and
flew them around a tree and

00:43:57.520 --> 00:43:58.635
did all the usual stuff.

00:43:58.635 --> 00:44:02.540
Then we started getting things, but
you know what, we couldn't get the tree.

00:44:02.540 --> 00:44:06.500
We couldn't do it, and
what we realised was in computer vision,

00:44:06.500 --> 00:44:10.040
a lot of what they do is use texture
to make up for missing geometry.

00:44:10.040 --> 00:44:12.707
So when you look at something, you feel
like the three geometry is there, but

00:44:12.707 --> 00:44:13.580
it's not really there.

00:44:13.580 --> 00:44:16.776
In fact, when you look at this picture
there, you might say that's a decent look

00:44:16.776 --> 00:44:19.257
of a 3D tree, but actually you're
not seeing a 3D tree at all.

00:44:19.257 --> 00:44:24.180
What you are looking at is a slide which
is projected on your 2D computer screen.

00:44:24.180 --> 00:44:27.110
It's completely 2D, there's no 3D
geometry in any of these pictures,

00:44:27.110 --> 00:44:29.200
actually nothing I've showed
you has any 3D geometry.

00:44:29.200 --> 00:44:31.470
You look at that tree,
there's no 3D geometry there.

00:44:31.470 --> 00:44:33.820
You think you see something in 3D,
but you don't.

00:44:33.820 --> 00:44:35.958
It's projected the 2D in your eyes, and

00:44:35.958 --> 00:44:40.189
your brain are doing shape from shading
other algorithms that make that feel 3D.

00:44:40.189 --> 00:44:42.340
And in fact,
whenever we do 3D reconstructions,

00:44:42.340 --> 00:44:43.890
of things in your computer vision and

00:44:43.890 --> 00:44:47.154
we think we're building 3D models,
we look at them on our computer screen.

00:44:47.154 --> 00:44:49.590
So we don't really have a good evaluation.

00:44:49.590 --> 00:44:52.520
So it turns out that we actually
need not just a new simulation

00:44:52.520 --> 00:44:56.220
to incorporate the data,
we need new data for the simulations.

00:44:56.220 --> 00:44:59.770
Typical computer vision doesn't really
construct the data that you need

00:44:59.770 --> 00:45:02.790
in order to go through and
articulate the tree up and

00:45:02.790 --> 00:45:05.210
build a multi value system
that can be simulated.

00:45:05.210 --> 00:45:08.395
It's good for looking at, it's good for
placing furniture, it's good for

00:45:08.395 --> 00:45:10.160
all these different things in a rough way.

00:45:10.160 --> 00:45:12.910
But you want to simulate all this,
find out the stress and

00:45:12.910 --> 00:45:16.302
strain on the floorboards from your
furniture and that sort of thing.

00:45:16.302 --> 00:45:20.369
Or actually put a bunch of cylinders into
this tree and have it bend in the wind and

00:45:20.369 --> 00:45:21.640
do its thing.

00:45:21.640 --> 00:45:26.420
It takes a lot more detailed geometry,
even new computer vision and

00:45:26.420 --> 00:45:29.470
machine learning algorithms in
order to just collect the data.

00:45:29.470 --> 00:45:33.618
She found herself an interesting spot
where we actually need really high

00:45:33.618 --> 00:45:37.774
fidelity geometry, the computer
vision really doesn't provide yet.

00:45:37.774 --> 00:45:41.121
And so we need not only the new simulation
technology take care of the data, but

00:45:41.121 --> 00:45:44.220
we need new ways of collecting
the data to provide to the simulation.

00:45:44.220 --> 00:45:47.210
And so it's a two-way thing,
that's what led me more recently into

00:45:48.450 --> 00:45:52.430
the artificial intelligence lab to work
with all these new applied mathematicians,

00:45:52.430 --> 00:45:53.910
which we call machine learning people.

00:45:55.640 --> 00:45:58.470
And the last slide just on wind inference,
it's one of the cool things you wanna do

00:45:58.470 --> 00:46:00.245
with the tree once we can
actually build the model.

00:46:00.245 --> 00:46:02.805
Once we can solve both
the problems I mentioned,

00:46:02.805 --> 00:46:05.996
then you can actually build this tree and
simulate the tree.

00:46:05.996 --> 00:46:09.534
And then video capture the tree, and
actually figure out how the wind

00:46:09.534 --> 00:46:12.402
is affecting the tree and
pull that wind off and use it for

00:46:12.402 --> 00:46:16.080
other trees or other models, or for
making movies and that sort thing.

00:46:16.080 --> 00:46:21.202
So I'll wrap up there at 10:50 and
we'll have ten minutes of questions.

00:46:21.202 --> 00:46:25.632
&gt;&gt; So we wanna head in to the Q&amp;A,
as reminder please type your questions

